<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="L-leucine degradation I" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="LEU-DEG2-PWY" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; |FRAME: LEU| is one of the three main branched chain amino acids (BCAAs), along with |FRAME: ILE| and |FRAME:VAL|. The catabolic pathways of the BCAAs can be divided into two sequential series of reactions, referred to as the common pathway and the distal pathway. The common pathway includes the enzymes |FRAME: CPLX-6941|, |FRAME: CPLX8J2-279|, and |FRAME: CPLX-6942|. These three enzymes catalyze the conversion of all three BCAAs to their respective acyl-CoA derivatives (|FRAME:METHACRYLYL-COA|, |FRAME:3-METHYL-CROTONYL-COA|, and |FRAME: CPD-1083| for |FRAME:VAL|, |FRAME:LEU|, and |FRAME:ILE|, respectively), though the intermediates formed by these enzymes are different for the different amino acids. The distal parts of the pathways are completely different for the three BCAAs, and comprise enzymes specific for each amino acid |CITS: [773366]|. This Leucine degradation pathway is wide spread, and is found in microorganisms, higher plants, and animals, including mammals. The catabolism of |FRAME: LEU| begins with its transamination to |FRAME:2K-4CH3-PENTANOATE|, followed by oxidative decarboxylation to |FRAME:ISOVALERYL-COA|. |FRAME:ISOVALERYL-COA| then undergoes a four-step process, involving dehydrogenation to |FRAME:3-METHYL-CROTONYL-COA|, carboxylation to |FRAME:TRANS-3-METHYL-GLUTACONYL-COA|, hydration to |FRAME: 3-HYDROXY-3-METHYL-GLUTARYL-COA|, and finally a thioester hydrolysis forming |FRAME: ACETYL-COA| and |FRAME:3-KETOBUTYRATE| |CITS: [4150714]|. Leucine degradation is different from the degradation of the other branched-chain amino acids, in that it includes a carboxylation step. The |FRAME: CARBON-DIOXIDE| fixing enzyme, |FRAME: CPLX66-17|, requires a biotin cofactor. In adipocytes, unlike other tissues, leucine is a significant precursor for fatty acid and sterol biosynthesis, especially in the presence of glucose |CITS: [13892210]|. Furthermore, leucine apparently serves as a source of nitrogen for synthesis of |FRAME: GLN| and, to lesser extents, |FRAME: GLT| and |FRAME: L-ALPHA-ALANINE|, which are released by adipocytes |CITS: [6773935]| and can serve as substrates for gluconeogenesis in liver and kidney |CITS: [6304077]|. It should be mentioned that alternative leucine degradation pathways exist. In one such pathway, documented in |FRAME: TAX-1509|, leucine is degraded to |FRAME:ISOBUTYRATE| and |FRAME: ACET| via |FRAME:CPD-2041| (see |FRAME:PWY-5075|). Additional pathways which are currently not characterized very well include the conversion of leucine to |FRAME: ACETONE| by members of the |FRAME:TAX-662| family |CITS: [10601206]|, and the catabolism of leucine to branched-chain fatty acids in |FRAME: TAX-1288| |CITS: [15078537]|. A portion of this leucine degradation pathway also participates in the final phase of the acyclic terpene utilization pathway, beginning at |FRAME: 3-METHYL-CROTONYL-COA| as shown in the pathway link from |FRAME: PWY-6672|. The acyclic terpene utilization pathway includes pathways |FRAME: PWY-6670|, |FRAME: PWY-6672| and this pathway, with its associated pathway link to |FRAME: ACETOACETATE-DEG-PWY|. In species of |FRAME:TAX-286| the enzymes of |FRAME: LEU|/|FRAME: ISOVALERATE| utilization are encoded in the &lt;i&gt;liuRABCDE&lt;/i&gt; gene cluster (reviewed in |CITS: [20490788]|).</Description>
